 ### Hessian-Vector Product via Central Differences
-**Definition:** An approximation of the Hessian-vector product $H(\theta)\mathbf{v}$ that avoids computing the full Hessian matrix [S5].
-**Step:** Evaluate the first-order gradient estimator at positions shifted from the current solution along the direction $\mathbf{v}$ by $\pm \epsilon$ [S5].
-**Formula:** Approximation using central differences: $H(\theta)\mathbf{v} \approx \lim_{\epsilon \to 0} \frac{g(\theta + \epsilon \mathbf{v}) - g(\theta - \epsilon \mathbf{v})}{2\epsilon}$. The smooth HVP kernel is estimated using directional central differences of the gradient estimator: $D_{HVP} \kappa(\tau) = \frac{D_G \kappa(\tau - \epsilon \mathbf{v}) - D_G \kappa(\tau + \epsilon \mathbf{v})}{2\epsilon}$ [S5].
-**Complexity:** Time complexity: $O(n)$ function evaluations without IS, $O(n)$ with IS, $O(1)$ with Aggregate IS. Space complexity for Aggregate IS is $O(n)$ [S5].
+#### definition
+- [S5] An approximation of the Hessian-vector product $H(\theta)v$ that avoids computing the full Hessian matrix.
+- [S5] Estimation of the Hessian-vector product $D_{HVP} = \nabla^2 v = \partial^2 / \partial\tau_i \partial\tau \cdot \partial/\partial\tau$, which is the directional gradient of the gradient, resulting in an n-dimensional vector.
+#### step
+- [S5] Evaluate the first-order gradient estimator at positions shifted from the current solution along the direction $v$ by $\pm \epsilon$.
+#### formula
+- [S5] Approximation using central differences: $H(\theta)v \approx \lim_{\epsilon \to 0} \frac{g(\theta + \epsilon v) - g(\theta - \epsilon v)}{2\epsilon}$.
+- [S5] The smooth HVP kernel is estimated using directional central differences of the gradient estimator: $D_{HVP} \kappa(\tau) = \frac{D_G \kappa(\tau - \epsilon v) - D_G \kappa(\tau + \epsilon v)}{2\epsilon}$.
+#### complexity
+- [S5] Time complexity: $O(n)$ function evaluations without IS, $O(n)$ with IS, $O(1)$ with Aggregate IS. Space complexity for Aggregate IS is $O(n)$.
 
 ## Background
-Finite difference methods are a cornerstone of numerical analysis for approximating derivatives of functions defined on discrete grids. In the context of volume rendering, they were adopted to provide surface normals for lighting calculations (such as Phong shading) when the volume data was represented as a dense grid of scalar values without explicit surface extraction. The central difference scheme is the most common due to its second-order accuracy and symmetry. Variants like the Sobel operator were introduced to mitigate noise inherent in discrete voxel data. The method is widely used in direct volume rendering and is a fundamental component in differentiable rendering pipelines for gradient-based optimization [S1, S5].
+Finite difference methods are foundational in numerical analysis and computer graphics. In volume rendering, they were adopted early to provide surface normals for shading when explicit geometry was unavailable. The distinction between simple central differences and more complex operators like Sobel or Zucker-Hummel emerged from the need to balance noise robustness against computational cost. The Zucker-Hummel operator, for instance, was developed to provide "softer and clearer" gradients by incorporating more spatial context (54 voxels vs 6) [S4, unofficial]. In differentiable rendering, finite differences are often used as a baseline for gradient estimation, with higher-order schemes like WENO5 introduced to reduce truncation error in smooth regions [S3]. The use of finite differences for Jacobian and Hessian estimation in deformation fields and optimization tasks (like Hessian-vector products) extends their utility beyond simple shading into physics simulation and inverse graphics [S1, S5].
 
